                <pb facs="0248" n="160" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg160"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Adee to Mr. Conger.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-09-02"><hi rendition="#italic">September 2,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 838.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch, No. 1607, of May 14 last, requesting instructions in regard to the
                    acceptance of the revised Chinese mining regulations, which, as you point out,
                    do not fulfill the provisions of the treaty with the United States.</p>
                <p>In reply I inclose herewith for your information and for your guidance in further
                    correspondence with the Chinese foreign office a copy of a letter from the
                    Acting Secretary of the Interior, inclosing copies of reports from the Acting
                    Director of the Geological Survey and the Acting Commissioner of the General
                    Land Office, embodying such suggestions as they deem practicable, with the view
                    to securing such amendments to the Chinese mining regulations as will bring them
                    more in harmony with our treaty with China and make them more practicable for
                    industrial and commercial purposes.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Alvey A. Adee</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>






